course: dictionary
lesson: 2787
title: Spotting disinformation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disinformation is false information spread on purpose to trick people. The important words are on purpose: someone chose to deceive. Look at this context: «Misinformation is false information shared by mistake; disinformation is false information spread on purpose.» The clue is on purpose, so disinformation is the deliberate kind of false information.
explain: Why would someone spread disinformation? Often it is to change what people think or how they vote. Sometimes many false messages are spread together, which is called a disinformation campaign. Fake accounts, bots and deepfake videos are some of the tools used to spread it.
explain: Context can help you here too. «Misinformation is false information shared by someone who thinks it is true; disinformation is made on purpose to trick.» A person who shares misinformation may believe it. A person who spreads disinformation knows it is false. A mistake that is fixed openly with a correction is not a lie.
fact: Disinformation is false information spread on purpose to trick people.
fact: Misinformation is false information shared by mistake, by someone who thinks it is true.
fact: A disinformation campaign is a planned effort to spread false information, often during wars and elections.
fact: To fight disinformation, check the source, look for other reports, and ask: who made this and why?
fact: You will often see the phrases «spread disinformation» and «fight disinformation» in the news.
exercise: «Misinformation is false information shared by mistake; disinformation is false information spread on purpose.» What makes disinformation different? | It is always true | *It is spread on purpose to trick people | It is always a picture | It is always shared by mistake
exercise: A fake account posts the same false story hundreds of times to change how people vote. What is this called? | A correction | A weather report | Misinformation shared by mistake | *Disinformation
exercise: You see a shocking story online. What is the best first step before sharing it? | Share it only if it makes you angry | Believe it because it is on a screen | *Look for other reports and ask who made it and why | Share it quickly before anyone else sees it
test: «A mistake fixed openly with a correction is not a lie.» Why is this true? | *Because the mistake was not made on purpose to trick people | Because corrections are always false | Because a lie is only a mistake | Because the person was trying to trick everyone
test: Which word means false information shared by mistake? | Disinformation | Propaganda campaign | *Misinformation | Deepfake
exam: A friend says, «A bot posted that the vote was cancelled, and it is all over the news, so it must be true.» What would you say to check it? | Ignore all news from now on | Share it at once because it is in the news | *Ask who made the post and why, and look for other reports before believing it | Believe it because a bot cannot lie
